  "raw_text": "Step 1: To find the domain of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{2x-7}{\\sqrt{x^2-5x+6}} \\), we need to ensure that the denominator is not zero and that the expression inside the square root is positive because the square root of a negative number is not defined in the set of real numbers.\n\nStep 2: Set up the inequality for the expression inside the square root to be greater than zero: \\( x^2 - 5x + 6 > 0 \\).\n\nStep 3: Factor the quadratic expression: \\( (x-2)(x-3) > 0 \\).\n\nStep 4: Determine the intervals where the product \\((x-2)(x-3)\\) is positive. The roots of the equation \\((x-2)(x-3) = 0\\) are \\(x = 2\\) and \\(x = 3\\). These roots divide the real number line into three intervals: \\((-\\infty, 2)\\), \\((2, 3)\\), and \\((3, \\infty)\\).\n\nStep 5: Test a point from each interval in the inequality \\((x-2)(x-3) > 0\\):\n- For \\(x \\in (-\\infty, 2)\\), choose \\(x = 0\\): \\((0-2)(0-3) = 6 > 0\\).\n- For \\(x \\in (2, 3)\\), choose \\(x = 2.5\\): \\((2.5-2)(2.5-3) = 0.5 \\cdot (-0.5) = -0.25 < 0\\).\n- For \\(x \\in (3, \\infty)\\), choose \\(x = 4\\): \\((4-2)(4-3) = 2 \\cdot 1 = 2 > 0\\).\n\nStep 6: The inequality \\((x-2)(x-3) > 0\\) holds true for \\(x \\in (-\\infty, 2) \\cup (3, \\infty)\\).\n\nFinal answer: \\boxed{(-\\infty, 2) \\cup (3, \\infty)}<|im_end|>",
  "reforward_peak_bytes": 15487654400,
